  "findings": {
    "headline": "Only one stage is super-linear. KG population scales as b = 0.9205 (sub-linear) and simple SPARQL retrieval is linear in the deployment regime (E7: b = 1.00 above 40k triples; the full-range b = 0.7279 is an artefact of fixed per-query overhead at small sizes). The 11-rule fixed point scales as b = 2.659 - between quadratic and cubic. The reasoning stage, not the network and not KG construction, governs how the pipeline scales.",
    "one_rule_dominates": "The reasoning cost is not spread across the rule base - it is one rule. R10 (Low-margin AND low-confidence top-2 disagreement) takes 12449 ms of a single pass, 95.1 % of the total, and enabling it multiplies the fixed-point cost by 19.0x (rules 1..9 cost 1.39 s; rules 1..10 cost 26.33 s). R10 is also the only rule producing feedback flags on Aruba (E2/E4), so the pipeline's entire feedback signal and the overwhelming majority of its reasoning cost come from the same single rule body. That is where any optimisation must go, and it is a concrete, actionable statement the complexity section can make.",
    "why_reasoning_is_worse": "Two effects compound. Individual rule bodies contain joins whose cost grows with the graph, and the fixed-point loop re-fires every rule after each round of newly asserted triples, so the graph the later passes run against is itself larger. Retrieval benchmarks miss this entirely, which is why a scalability result built only on SELECT queries understates the cost of the deployed pipeline.",
    "dominant_rules": "Single-pass cost is concentrated: rules [10, 8, 4] account for 97 % of one pass. Optimisation effort should target those bodies rather than the rule base as a whole.",
    "cost_vs_rule_count": "Cost does not grow with rule count in any smooth way: rules 1..9 together cost 1.39 s, and the full base costs 26.29 s. Nearly all of the difference is one rule. A complexity model linear in |R| would therefore mispredict badly - what matters is which bodies are in the base, not how many."
  }
